    "response": ".\n\nTo find \\( f'(-2) \\) for the function \\( f(x) = -3x^4 + x^3 - x^2 - 6x - 6 \\), we first need to compute the derivative of \\( f(x) \\).\n\nThe derivative of a polynomial is found by applying the power rule, which states that the derivative of \\( x^n \\) is \\( nx^{n-1} \\). Applying this rule to each term in \\( f(x) \\), we get:\n\n\\[\nf'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^4) + \\frac{d}{dx}(x^3) + \\frac{d}{dx}(-x^2) + \\frac{d}{dx}(-6x) + \\frac{d}{dx}(-6)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each derivative individually, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^4) = -12x^3\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^3) = 3x^2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx}(-x^2) = -2x\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx}(-6x) = -6\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dx}(-6) = 0\n\\]\n\nAdding these derivatives together, we get:\n\n\\[\nf'(x) = -12x^3 + 3x^2 - 2x - 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to evaluate \\( f'(-2) \\). We substitute \\( x = -2 \\) into the derivative:\n\n\\[\nf'(-2) = -12(-2)^3 + 3(-2)^2 - 2(-2) - 6\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate each term step by step:\n\n\\[\n-12(-2)^3 = -12(-8) = 96\n\\]\n\\[\n3(-2)^2 = 3(4) = 12\n\\]\n\\[\n-2(-2) = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n-6 = -6\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together, we get:\n\n\\[\nf'(-2) = 96 + 12 + 4 - 6 = 106\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f'(-2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{106}\\). The correct choice is \\(\\boxed{D}\\).",
